---
import ChronicleLayout from '../../layouts/ChronicleLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleHeader.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/chronicle/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/chronicle/Figure.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/chronicle/SectionHeading.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/chronicle/AtAGlance.astro';
import Practical from '../../components/chronicle/Practical.astro';
import PracticalSection from '../../components/chronicle/PracticalSection.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/chronicle/FurtherReading.astro';
import ArticleFAQ from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleFAQ.astro';
import type { ChronicleMeta } from '../../components/chronicle/types';
import { SIZES_MODERN_WIDE } from '../../lib/responsive-image-config.mjs';

const meta: ChronicleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Things to Do in Mendoza: A First-Hand Guide',
  headline: 'Best Things to Do in Mendoza',
  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in Mendoza, from its formal streets and parks to Cerro de la Gloria and a separate day in wine country.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-mendoza/',
  ogImage: '/images/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/mendoza-tiled-park-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary';
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Argentina', 'Mendoza', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · City and wine country"
    title="Best Things to Do in Mendoza"
    standfirst="Mendoza is known for Malbec, bodegas and vineyards against the Andes. But the city earns attention before the wine day begins: through formal streets, mature greenery, monuments and the foothill edge beyond them."
    stats={[
      { num: '4', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Nights' },
      { num: '2', label: 'Registers' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Mendoza arrives in the imagination as wine country: Malbec, long rows of vines,
          bodegas and the Andes waiting beyond them. The city deserves a different first
          reading. Its formal streets, mature trees, parks and monuments give the visit a
          shape before any vineyard appears.
        </p>
        <p>
          The traveller spent three nights in Mendoza between 23 and 26 September 2026,
          arriving from Santiago before continuing east towards Iguazú. That position on
          the journey makes the city easy to underestimate. It is a base for wine country,
          certainly, but it is also a planned urban landscape with its own scale and edge.
        </p>
        <p>
          The strongest visit gives the two registers separate time: the city first, the
          vineyards afterwards. For the full dated sequence, continue to the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">three-day Mendoza itinerary</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A tiled public park in Mendoza with a central monument and mature trees."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Mendoza’s formal greenery establishes the city before the vineyards begin."
      priority
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="glance"
    title="Choose your Mendoza"
    meta={[
      {
        label: 'City',
        value: 'Central Mendoza and Parque General San Martín.',
      },
      {
        label: 'City edge',
        value: 'Cerro de la Gloria, San Vicente Park and the foothill geography beyond the centre.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Wine day',
        value: 'A separate day among Mendoza’s named wine-country stops.',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <AtAGlance
    id="mendoza-glance"
    title="Mendoza at a Glance"
    meta={[
      { label: 'Best first stop', value: 'Central Mendoza for the formal streets, mature trees and the city before the vineyards.' },
      { label: 'Best green space', value: 'Parque General San Martín, where Mendoza’s planned landscape becomes visible at scale.' },
      { label: 'Best for geography', value: 'Cerro de la Gloria and the city edge, where the foothills begin to enter the urban experience.' },
      { label: 'Best full-day experience', value: 'Wine country, kept separate from the city so neither half of Mendoza has to compete with the other.' },
    ]}
    daysHeading="Four Ways into Mendoza"
    days={[
      { num: 'Central Mendoza', detail: 'Streets · Civic life · Urban form', text: 'The city before the bodegas. Broad streets, mature trees and public space make the centre worth walking rather than simply passing through.' },
      { num: 'Parque General San Martín', detail: 'Green space · Monuments · City scale', text: 'Mendoza’s strongest urban landscape. The park gives the city room and makes its relationship with trees and formal planning easier to read.' },
      { num: 'Cerro de la Gloria', detail: 'Monument · Foothills · Geography', text: 'The point where Mendoza begins to meet the land beyond it. Best for understanding the city in relation to the foothills and Andes.' },
      { num: 'Wine Country', detail: 'Bodegas · Vineyards · Full-day excursion', text: 'A second register of Mendoza rather than the whole story. Give it its own day instead of squeezing vineyards into the city route.' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter id="central" number="I." name="Walk Central Mendoza" epithet="Formal streets, public space and the city before the vineyards" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Central Mendoza is where the city makes its own case. Broad streets, mature trees,
        formal buildings and the movement between them produce a place that feels planned
        around public space rather than arranged only for passing through. The university
        area belongs to this wider reading of the centre: another part of the city’s civic
        life, not a separate attraction to be collected.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the Mendoza that disappears when the city is treated as a transfer point
        for wineries. The streets connect the architecture, parks and monuments into one
        urban experience. Leave room for that connective tissue. The city is not merely
        what happens between the bodegas.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A broad Mendoza street corner shaded by mature trees."
      width={2048}
      height={1152}
      caption="Mendoza’s streets make the city feel planned around shade, distance and public movement."
    />

    <Chapter id="san-martin" number="II." name="Spend Time in Parque General San Martín" epithet="The green structure that gives Mendoza room" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Parque General San Martín is the strongest city recommendation because it makes
        Mendoza’s green character visible at scale. The park is not a brief interruption
        in the urban fabric; it gives the city a wider public landscape of trees, formal
        routes and monuments. The streets of central Mendoza begin to make more sense
        once the park is understood as part of the same design rather than as an optional
        patch of greenery.
      </p>
      <p>
        The park changes the rhythm of the visit without requiring the city to disappear.
        Architecture gives way to landscape, then returns. That movement is one of
        Mendoza’s defining pleasures: the city keeps its formal shape while making room
        for trees, open ground and the monuments that lead the eye towards the edge beyond.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="gloria" number="III." name="Cerro de la Gloria and the City Edge" epithet="A monument, a supporting park and the city meeting the foothills" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Cerro de la Gloria changes the geographical scale of Mendoza. The monument and
        its foothill setting pull the visit away from the level streets and towards the
        land that surrounds the city. The Andes are no longer a distant idea behind the
        urban plan; the edge of that landscape enters the day directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its importance is therefore both civic and geographical. Cerro de la Gloria is
        not simply another high point to add to a list of views. It is where Mendoza’s
        built order meets the larger terrain beyond it, and where the city’s parks,
        monuments and foothills begin to read as one continuous setting.
      </p>
      <p>
        San Vicente Park belongs to the same wider movement as Cerro de la Gloria: away
        from the formal centre and towards the city’s outer geography. It earns a place
        here not as a standalone attraction with a separate checklist, but as part of the
        way Mendoza opens beyond its central streets.
      </p>
      <p>
        Together, the monument and park show Mendoza opening beyond its central streets,
        with green public space and the land beyond the city forming part of the destination.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="wine-country" number="IV." name="Give Wine Country Its Own Day" epithet="The vineyards as a second register of Mendoza" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Wine country deserves its own day because it is a different register of Mendoza,
        not because it makes the city incidental. The day included Carmelo Patti, Bodega
        Viamonte, Cecchin, Pasrai, Vistandes and Bodega Esencia 1870. These producers give
        the wine-country side of the stay a clear shape without needing to be turned into
        a ranking.
      </p>
      <p>
        The useful distinction is simple. Give the vineyards time to be vineyards, and
        give the city time to be a city. Mendoza works best when the two experiences are
        allowed to stand beside one another rather than compete for the whole visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Mendoza — Things to Do" detail="City + wine country" detailIcon="🧭">
      <PracticalSection label="Shortest route to a great first visit" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>One day</strong> — Walk central Mendoza, spend time in Parque General San Martín and add Cerro de la Gloria if the day still has room.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Two days</strong> — Keep one day for the city, park and foothill edge, then give the second entirely to wine country.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Three days</strong> — Follow the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">complete Mendoza itinerary</a>, with enough time for both registers to breathe rather than compressing them together.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>If you only choose three things</strong> — Central Mendoza, Parque General San Martín and one full wine-country day.</div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="What each part of Mendoza does best" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>City character:</strong> Central Mendoza.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Green space:</strong> Parque General San Martín.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Landscape and geography:</strong> Cerro de la Gloria.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Wine:</strong> Mendoza’s vineyard country.</div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not reduce Mendoza to wine.</strong> The city itself deserves time before the vineyard day begins.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Keep the wine day separate.</strong> Bodegas and vineyards work better as a full excursion than as fragments attached to a city route.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>The city edge needs transport planning.</strong> Cerro de la Gloria and San Vicente Park sit outside the easiest central walking pattern.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Leave room between stops.</strong> Mendoza works better when the city, parks and wine country are allowed to feel distinct rather than stacked into one oversized day.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <div class="narrative">
      <SectionHeading id="priorities">What Deserves Priority</SectionHeading>
      <div class="narrative prose">
        <p>
          <strong>With one day:</strong> begin in central Mendoza, spend time in Parque
          General San Martín and add Cerro de la Gloria if the practical arrangements suit
          the day.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>With two days:</strong> keep one day for the city, parks and foothill edge,
          then give the second to wine country. San Vicente Park fits into the wider
          city-edge day rather than needing a separate full-day plan.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>With three days:</strong> follow the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">complete Mendoza itinerary</a> for the dated sequence, then use the extra time to let the city and wine country breathe at their own pace.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <SectionHeading id="practical">Practical guidance</SectionHeading>
      <div class="narrative prose">
        <p>
          Check current access, transport and opening arrangements before travelling,
          particularly when building the city-and-edge day around Cerro de la Gloria and
          San Vicente Park.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the wider journey, Mendoza sits between Santiago and the onward route towards
          Iguazú. The <a href="/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/">24 Days in South America itinerary</a> places the city within that larger crossing, while <a href="/mendoza-beyond-wine/">Mendoza Beyond Wine</a> stays with the parks, streets and monuments that make the city worth the pause.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <SectionHeading id="closing">A City and a Wine Country</SectionHeading>
      <div class="narrative prose">
        <p>
          Mendoza works because the city and the vineyards do not need to compete. The
          trees, parks, formal streets, monument and foothill edge earn their own day; the
          bodegas then become a second register rather than the only reason to arrive.
        </p>
        <p>
          The city deserves the pause before the road continues east.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">But the road is ever onward.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Mendoza"
      cards={[
        { href: '/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/', region: 'Argentina · Mendoza', title: '3 Days in Mendoza: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full route through the city, parks, foothill landmarks and wine country.', meta: ['Itinerary', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/mendoza-beyond-wine/', region: 'Argentina · Mendoza', title: 'Mendoza Beyond Wine: Exploring the City Beyond the Wineries', excerpt: 'A focused account of the parks, streets, monuments and foothill edge that make Mendoza more than a wine base.', meta: ['City guide', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/', region: 'South America · Argentina', title: '24 Days in South America', excerpt: 'The wider journey from Santiago through Mendoza and onward towards Iguazú.', meta: ['Pillar itinerary', '2026'] },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
  <ArticleFAQ page="best-things-to-do-in-mendoza" />
</ChronicleLayout>
